/*!

\page working-with-styles.html
\title Working with Styles and Style Packages

\section1 Styles and Style Packages

Union Styles are directories with specific contents. The directory name is used
as a style ID and each directory is expected to contain a \c{contents} directory
and a single \c{metadata.json} file. The metadata file contains things like the
name, authors and other information about the style. The contents directory
contains input-specific files. For CSS based styles, it is expected to contain
another directory \c{css}, which is expected to contain at least a single file,
\c{style.css} which is the main file read by the CSS input.

To make it easier to distribute these styles, a style can be packages into a
\i{style package}. Style packages are .tar.gz archives using the \c{.unionstyle}
as extension.

To work with styles and style packages, Union comes with a command-line tool
called \c{union-styletool}. It is recommended to use this tool to work with
styles rather than trying to manipulate them manually.

\section1 Creating a New Style

\c{union-styletool create} will interactively create an empty style, with
provided metadata. The style will be created in the current directory by default,
but this can be customised with the \c{--output-path <output-path>} argument.

A created style will always use the CSS input and have the following structure:

\code
<style id>/
  contents/
    css/
      style.css
    images/
      README.md
  metadata.json
\endcode

\c{metadata.json} contains the metadata provided when creating the style.

The \c{contents/css/} directory contains a single \c{style.css} file which is
read by the CSS input as entry point. It is recommended to not place any CSS
rules in this file. Instead, you should create additional CSS files for the
rules and place them next to \c{style.css}, then use the CSS \c{@import}
directive to import them. This prevents the main \c{style.css} file from
becoming too large and hard to work with.

The \c{contents/images/} directory is used for any images your style uses. You
can reference them from your CSS file as relative URLs like
\c{url(../images/image.svg)}.

To find out more about how to write CSS for a Union style, please refer to
the \l{css-styling-applications.html}{Styling Applications with CSS} page.

\section1 Installing, Uninstalling and Updating Styles

After creating a style, you will need to install it for Union to be able to use
it. You can install a style using the \c{union-styletool install} command, with
the path to the style you want to install provided. This will install the style
for the local user. This command supports both unpackaged and packaged styles.
You can then select the style from your system's style selection page or, if you
want to test a single application, you can use the
\c{UNION_STYLE_NAME=<style id>} environment variable to override the system's
style.

Should you wish to uninstall the style again, you can use the
\c{union-styletool uninstall} command. This will uninstall the style if it can
be uninstalled. Note that system-provided styles cannot be uninstall by
union-styletool and you will need to use your system's tools for that.

Finally, if you have a new version of a style, use the
\c{union-styletool update} command to update the style. This command will check
if the style is installed, and if it is and a newer version has been provided,
it will update it.

\section1 Packaging a Style for Redistribution

If you are happy with your style, you can use the \c{union-styletool package}
command to package an existing style into a redistributable package that others
can install. You can pass this command an ID of an installed style or the path
to a style that is not installed. Once finished, the working directory will
contain a file called \c{<style id>.unionstyle} that you can then redistribute.

\section1 Other Commands

\c{union-styletool} also has a \c{list} command which will list all installed
styles and a \c{inspect} command to inspect whether a given style is a correct
style.

*/
